# Pi is a Node project inside a Rust workspace. Nothing installed or generated
# here may reach git: ga and tars both track vendored trees, and their
# structural-quality metrics are consequently dominated by node_modules rather
# than by their own code. ix has stayed clean; this experiment must not be what
# breaks that.
node_modules/

# Pi writes sessions under ~/.pi by default; PI_AGENT_DIR points it here instead
# so a spike leaves no trace in the user's home. Session transcripts carry
# prompts, file contents and provider responses -- never commit them.
.pi/
sessions/

# Provider credentials.
.env
.env.*

# Ephemeral git worktree used as Pi's sandbox. Pi's `bash` and `write` tools can
# mutate anything they can reach, and Pi ships no path restriction, so probes
# run against a throwaway checkout rather than the real working tree. Diffing
# the worktree afterwards is also how we see what the agent actually touched.
worktree/

# Raw event streams: bulky, reproducible, and transcript-shaped -- they embed
# prompts and the contents of every file the agent read. The normalised artifact
# in state/*.second-opinion.json is the reviewable record; this is local
# evidence, regenerated by re-running the probe.
state/raw/
